+# Aux Vault request-scoped WebAuthn adapter
+
+Original Aux provider selected at Chromium155's validated RP continuation, after
+security context, permissions policy, ancestor, RP and TLS checks. It does not
+attach webAuthenticationProxy globally and does not replace renderer credential
+APIs. A native tab-modal chooser identifies the actual requesting origin. Other
+providers resume the existing request delegate and normal discovery flow.
+
+Only modal, same-origin, non-payment, non-remote, nonvirtual calls offer this
+provider. Conditional/ambient/immediate mediation remains with existing
+providers. Aux supports P-256/ES256, none attestation, discoverable credentials
+and credProps; unsupported extensions/attestation fail explicitly in the Vault
+service. Native completion uses Chromium's own strict response parser, verifies
+exact client data, RP hash, ID, UP/UV flags, no backup flags and credential
+allow/exclude lists. It restores the requesting WebContents' focus, rechecks
+lifetime, and preserves Chromium's final focus check.
+
+`BeginCreate/BeginGet` retain a WeakDocumentPtr, actual Profile pointer, caller
+and top origins, and a random request UUID. Content owns a ScopedClosureRunner
+which abandons this exact request on abort/teardown. Requests expire in two
+minutes and complete at most once. Private Vault API `getRequest` returns an
+installation-signed envelope only while the profile's actual disk account gate
+is configured, onboarded and unlocked. It signs `Aux Passkey Request v1\n` plus
+the exact raw JSON; this domain cannot serve as a daemon authentication response.
+The envelope includes account, profile, mode, native Profile lifetime nonce,
+frame process/routing identity, RP, origins, native clientDataJSON and options.
+`complete` rereads the profile gate, verifies the same bound account and exact
+live document, then consumes the callback. `reject` cancels it. These private
+functions have a Vault-only extension allowlist and native ID check.
+
+The consent popup uses `index.html#/passkeys/<UUID>` in the same actual Profile.
+Normal mode stores encrypted credentials through the dedicated Vault collection;
+private mode uses RAM keys bound to the signed lifetime nonce. A ProfileObserver
+abandons pending callbacks and sends authenticated Chromium-only retirement for
+the exact private nonce when the Profile is destroyed. The cleanup body contains
+only account/profile/random session identity; it uses the live local daemon
+credential and validated AUX_PORT loopback endpoint, with a five-second bound.
+Shutdown also destroys the daemon RAM state. Vault worker cleanup and pruning
+older signed epochs provide additional cleanup without adopting a reused Profile
+pointer or numeric frame ID.
+
+Compilation target is enabled `out/AuxAside`, with `enable_aux_browser=true` and
+`enable_aux_shell=false`, under `/tmp/aux-build.lock`. Six focused native objects
+cover broker, cleanup, private API, Chrome delegate, public delegate and content
+request continuation. No owner Profile, Keychain or RP credentials are used.
+Full linked disposable-profile registration/assertion observation is a separate
+integration acceptance step; compilation does not establish that evidence.
+
+Sources: approved docs/spec/daemon/services.md Vault behavior and
+native/apis-core.md A0 private capability boundary; public WebAuthn3 and upstream
+Chromium155 authenticator_common_impl, web_authentication_delegate,
+client_data_json and value_conversions interfaces. No reference artifacts used.
